Singapore's tourism rebounds strongly, with inbound tourists up 19 times from 2021
（Combined report on November 11th）The Singapore Tourism Board recently released that the number of international tourists visiting Singapore in 2023 is expected to be 12 million to 14 million, which will bring about about 1.8 billion to 2.1 billion Singapore dollars in tourism income; the number of tourists and tourism income will recover to about 67% to 75% of the level before the COVID-19 pandemic in 2019; and the possibility of the tourism industry recovering to its full potential in 2024 is very high.
The Singapore tourism market is gradually restarted and recovered in 2022.
The latest statistics released by the Singapore Tourism Board showed that the number of tourists entering the country in 2022 increased 19 times from 330,600 in 2021 to 6.3 million, exceeding the earlier forecast of 4 million to 6 million, and recovered to 33% of the pre-pandemic level.
Tourism revenue in Singapore for January to September 2022 was S$8.96 billion, and it is expected that tourism revenue for the full year of 2022 will be between S$13.8 billion and S$14.3 billion, up sixfold to sevenfold from S$1.89 billion in 2021, which has already recovered to around 50% to 52% of pre-COVID-19 levels.
Singapore government has taken a series of measures to accelerate the recovery of tourism.
In April 2022, the government announced a nearly 500 million yuan (US$1.4 billion) allocation to stimulate the recovery of tourism.
The Tourism Board announced earlier this month that it would allocate RM110 million from the budget over the next two years to attract more business and leisure activities to lure tourists back.
To make health and wellness one of Singapore's tourism highlights, the Singapore Tourism Board has launched an intentional call for proposals on 23 November 2022 to develop a health and wellness attraction on the southern coast of Singapore.
Singapore Tourism Board's Executive Director of Sports and Wellness Tourism, Wang Lingling, said that in recent years, more and more tourists have put their health as the first consideration in all aspects.
The development of a health and wellness resort in the Hainan district of Singapore, which has the Hanging Bay Gardens and Hanging Bay Dams, is expected to contribute to Singapore's tourism and economy, allowing tourists to spend and stay longer in Singapore.
Singapore Tourism Board chief executive, Chang Jit Koon, said that health and wellness will be an important part of the country's tourism product in the coming few years.
The pandemic has raised public awareness and demand for physical and mental health.
For this reason, the Tourism Board has been promoting health and wellness programs to the local public and international tourists for the past two years.
In June 2022, the Singapore Tourism Board held the first Health and Wellness Festival, providing more than 130 different types of health activities and experiences for the public.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences for visitors.
In addition to the newly opened Singapore Children's Museum, the Avatar World at the Bay of Plenty Gardens, the Central Beach Market in Santa Tausa, the newly added Pygmy Exploration Pygmy Exhibition Hall at the Museum of Arts and Sciences, and the newly built outdoor theatre at the Night Wildlife Park, the tourism department will support the development and renewal of projects such as the Flying Bird Park at the World of Wildlife, making Singapore an attractive international travel destination.
According to the plan, Singapore will develop industry standards for sustainable development of meetings, exhibitions and incentive tourism in 2023, with particular focus on waste management and carbon emissions reduction issues, further optimizing the travel environment.
The recovery of Singapore's tourism industry has accelerated the recovery of the aviation industry.
Data show that as of 18 December 2022, passenger traffic at Changi Airport has recovered strongly, with up to 150,000 daily and about 1 million weekly passengers, equivalent to about 75% of the pre-COVID-19 level, and the number of airline employees has also returned to about 90% of pre-epidemic levels.
Data showed that from January to October this year, Singapore received 23.6 million tourists, which is equivalent to 42% of the pre-epidemic level, and completed 170,000 flights, which is equivalent to 53% of the pre-epidemic level.
The tourism industry is recovering quickly, which is also driving the recovery of accommodation, transportation, dining and shopping industries.
Singapore's government and tourism industry have high hopes for the Chinese market.
Khoo Suet-Dar, Assistant Director of the International Department of the Singapore Tourism Board, said that a survey of China's tourism platform showed that Singapore's safety index ranked among the top three in the world, which is one of the reasons why Singapore is so popular among Chinese tourists.
Before the outbreak of the new coronavirus, about 3.6 million Chinese tourists visited Singapore every year.
As China relaxes its border policies, it is expected that the number of Chinese tourists will recover to 30% to 60% of the number in 2019.
Koo Suet-dann said that the Tourism Board will closely monitor the signals released by China for outbound tourism policies to respond to the changing needs of tourists.
Singapore Tourism Board will also strengthen partnerships with Chinese online travel platforms and travel agencies, and prepare to welcome Chinese tourists in overseas markets.
Looking ahead to 2023, Singapore Tourism Board is optimistic about the tourism market, believing that as long as global economic conditions do not experience significant downturns and there are no new large-scale outbreaks of deadly pandemics, the pace of tourism recovery will be faster than expected.
A quick guide to the Internet Finance
This book is very shallow; it can be considered an introductory course that will help you quickly understand the broad picture of internet finance but to really delve deeper, you need to follow the map and dig on your own.
And it was written earlier, and the content of the later WeChat and Alipay subsidy war to cultivate user habits has not been mentioned. The proportion of third-party payment is probably not as small as it is mentioned in the book now.
The bank has also been criticized for its lack of transparency and transparency, and the fact that many people are taking their phones out to eat, and businessmen are abandoning the bank because of the high bank liquidity.
But still, I am very grateful to the author for writing this introductory book; it is evident that the author has had many years of financial observation experience and is familiar with both domestic and international financial development histories, which allows him/her to write in such an easy-to-understand manner.
It is easy to write something high and big, but it is difficult to write something that is easy to understand.
