Not really recommending it.
I read it a long time ago and I happened to see the feeling of reading it from memory.
This book is not a short story collection, but a collection of fragments of love in life.
The tone of the book is very plain, it is the kind of plain tone that is as plain as water, the use of words and phrases is to the extreme in pursuit of simplicity, and the plot is as plain as the streets in the afternoon of a summer day.
Each short story is a silhouette of a small period in ordinary life, without beginning or end.
Finally gathered together, they named it "When We Talk About Love, What Are We Talking About?"
I think that this collection of novels, and its title, is like a performance art, the author wants to express that love is more than a sad scene, the sea of dead stone in urban legends, not just your mother's paint, not only is love more than that and should not be represented, love is also life like fresh water, dry and tasteless day by day, but also helplessness of life, escape from reality, the silence of the future.
Reading this book, you may think of the spouses you have been fighting with, the bad marriages of people you know, and the unhappy feelings you have for each other.
This is exactly why I don't recommend reading it, from an aesthetic point of view, the writing and the plot of the book are completely tasteless, unless your life is a surreal or sweet as living in a candy castle, you have to read it to feel weird and boring.
In terms of depth of thought, the daily scenes do not make one's eyes light up or expand one's horizons (unlike the love story in One Hundred Years of Solitude by Marquez), and the brief scenes do not discuss any issues, such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of love?
What causes the breakdown of the protagonist's marriage?
So I don't recommend reading this book, it doesn't matter if you read it or not.
The author's ideas are expressed more through technique and form than through plot, which is one of the reasons why the book is not as good to read.
